WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.520
You can't pause a movie on the big screen,&nbsp;
but don't you wish you could sometimes? To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.840
get a better look at Batman's first big&nbsp;
moment? Or to see all the details in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.240
the Riddler's hideout? Keep watching for&nbsp;
more pause-worthy "The Batman" moments.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.960
The opening sequence to "The Batman" will easily&nbsp;
be one of the most talked about scenes, just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:27.200
because of the foreboding tone it sets. It's akin&nbsp;
to the swelling dread one feels as Michael Myers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:38.640
stalks his prey in a "Halloween" movie. The victim&nbsp;
here is Gotham's mayor, who sits unsuspecting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.240
the privacy of his luxurious abode. As the mayor&nbsp;
views the public reaction to his own campaign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:49.600
being broadcast on television, he's entirely&nbsp;
unaware of the danger that lurks in the shadows.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.760
Hauntingly, the figure of the Riddler is seen&nbsp;
lying in wait not even five feet away from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.280
intended victim. Paul Dano's acting skill is&nbsp;
on full display here — he can make your skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.680
crawl with just the movement of his eyes.&nbsp;
There's a lot to savor in this sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.640
particularly for viewers who have an appreciation&nbsp;
for horror themes and a creepy atmosphere.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.400
The arrival of a film's title character&nbsp;
is often cause for a grand entrance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.120
especially for a character as popular as&nbsp;
Batman. After the film moves on from its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.800
horrifying introduction of the Riddler, Robert&nbsp;
Pattinson's Bruce Wayne speaks to the audience via&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.520
a sullen voiceover that underscores the&nbsp;
bleak and depressing outlook of a city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:37.520
riddled with crime. While he's unsure whether he's&nbsp;
actually doing any good, there's one thing he is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.880
certain of: Criminals feel uneasy when the Bat&nbsp;
Signal shines in the sky, and for good reason.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:53.120
"When that light hits the sky, it's&nbsp;
not just a call. It's a warning."

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.240
On a subway, a gang that looks to be inspired&nbsp;
by the Joker, based on their face paint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.920
targets an unlucky soul and begins assaulting the&nbsp;
poor man. Quickly, the sounds of footsteps can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.640
heard from within the dark tunnel opposite&nbsp;
the attackers. The gang stops and looks on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.600
waiting to see what emerges from the dark. As&nbsp;
Batman steps out into the light, he provides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:19.360
a demonstration of his fear-inducing tactics by&nbsp;
brutalizing the gang's apparent leader in front of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:26.000
them. Some stay and fight, while others flee. It's&nbsp;
a visceral display of Batman's rage, and the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.840
earns its place as a crowd-pleasing&nbsp;
starting point for Batman's journey.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.680
Long before "The Batman" arrived in theaters,&nbsp;
audiences knew for quite some time that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:41.200
Penguin was going to have a role in the film.&nbsp;
Given that the story takes place early in Batman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:47.040
crime fighting career, the Penguin isn't quite&nbsp;
yet the crime lord comics readers know him to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:52.560
He operates the Iceberg Lounge under the&nbsp;
mob-backed supervision of Carmine Falcone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:58.000
Colin Farrell absolutely disappears into the&nbsp;
Penguin beneath prosthetics and make-up work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.440
Despite his altered appearance, he manages&nbsp;
to nail the mannerisms of an egomaniacal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.320
crook vying for power. All the fun begins&nbsp;
when the Dark Knight heads to the Iceberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:14.000
and questions the Penguin regarding his possible&nbsp;
associations with the recently deceased mayor.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.680
Seeing the Penguin and knowing that Colin Farrell&nbsp;
is buried beneath that grungy exterior and slimy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:24.560
attitude is quite impressive. Viewers might&nbsp;
find themselves scanning the character for any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:29.920
identifiable aspect of the Farrell they remember,&nbsp;
but they'll be hard-pressed to find anything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.800
Despite his own penchant for crime and corruption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:36.800
the Penguin positions himself as one of&nbsp;
the film's more interesting characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.000
even though he has little to do with&nbsp;
the burgeoning threat of the Riddler.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.680
Throughout the film, the Riddler taunts the&nbsp;
residents of Gotham with each kill. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:51.120
he believes that he's doing the city a service&nbsp;
by rooting out corruption. He also wants to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.560
Batman's attention, hoping that the vigilante will&nbsp;
side with him in his crusade to punish the wicked.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.560
"I think I'm his last target."

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.240
The news regularly shows recorded&nbsp;
threats that the Riddler has posted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.680
in an effort to gain clout and&nbsp;
attention throughout his campaign.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.840
When the police finally detain the&nbsp;
Riddler, they investigate his apartment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.640
which is littered with newspaper clippings&nbsp;
and details of Gotham's past and present.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.600
The Riddler's cluttered abode contains secrets&nbsp;
and details that many fans will certainly want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:25.360
to pause and scour for clues to a larger world&nbsp;
beyond Gotham City. When the Riddler discusses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:31.760
the affluent Wayne and Arkham families, eagle-eyed&nbsp;
fans can see a clipping referencing Edward Elliot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:35.840
another famous Gotham City aristocrat.&nbsp;
Those who remember the Elliot family from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:39.680
the "Batman" comics will recognize&nbsp;
this as a nod to Tommy Elliot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:43.760
Bruce's former childhood friend, who&nbsp;
grows up to become the villainous Hush.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.720
A Batman movie just wouldn't be&nbsp;
an epic Dark Knight tale without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.720
a thrilling Batmobile sequence.&nbsp;
"The Batman" doesn't disappoint.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:58.160
Batman unveils his monster machine with a&nbsp;
thunderous rumble. As the Dark Knight pursues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.280
the Penguin in an effort to learn whether he's an&nbsp;
informant in a case the Riddler keeps referencing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:07.600
he has to deal with the dangerous criminal's&nbsp;
collateral damage. The Penguin enters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.640
the freeway driving the wrong direction,&nbsp;
dodging cars and causing massive pile-ups.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.600
At one point, the Penguin causes a collision&nbsp;
that includes a truck carrying liquid fuel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.240
Looking in his rear view mirror, he believes&nbsp;
the Batman was either forced to stop his pursuit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.960
or taken out in the explosion of the&nbsp;
truck's trailer. He hollers for joy…

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:31.840
"I got you! Ha ha ha! I got you!"

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:35.760
…but his excitement is short-lived,&nbsp;
as the Batmobile comes careening out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:39.520
of the flames much to the Penguin's&nbsp;
horror. Hot-rod enthusiasts who love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:43.840
cars with some serious muscle and fans&nbsp;
of the Batmobile will definitely want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:48.560
admire the stunt work and thrilling visuals&nbsp;
on display during this harrowing sequence.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:55.440
The relationship between Batman and Catwoman has&nbsp;
long been one of the most entertainingly complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:00.240
in comics. Although it has yet to be fully&nbsp;
explored on the big screen, audiences have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:04.880
had several opportunities to see the Bat and the&nbsp;
Cat in action. Michelle Pfeiffer's [FY-fur] take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:10.080
on the role in 1992's "Batman Returns," is&nbsp;
unforgettable, and Anne Hathaway put her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.120
own spin on the character when Selina Kyle fought&nbsp;
side by side with the Caped Crusader in "The Dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.720
Knight Rises." "The Batman," however, gives us&nbsp;
what might be the most comics-accurate portrayal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:26.080
of Catwoman yet, with Zoë [ZOH-ee] Kravitz&nbsp;
donning the leather bodysuit and cat-burglar mask.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:30.160
When Batman follows Selina to find out&nbsp;
how she's involved in the case at hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:35.680
he discovers her breaking into a safe, commenting&nbsp;
on her uncanny ability to crack them so easily.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:39.440
Startled, she turns around and starts&nbsp;
fighting him. The sequence fits neatly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:43.760
into the narrative while doubling as thrilling&nbsp;
fan service for everyone in the audience who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:47.360
been looking forward to seeing these&nbsp;
characters come together onscreen again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.640
If viewers look closely, they can even see that&nbsp;
Selina is sporting some wickedly long fingernails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.480
prime for catlike scratch attacks.&nbsp;
Thankfully, the clash is brief.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:01.840
Just as they have so many times before,&nbsp;
Batman and Catwoman quickly form an alliance.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.480
As many viewers will no doubt suspect,&nbsp;
the Riddler's capture isn't the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:11.520
but it does set up his endgame. Despite his&nbsp;
arrest, he has power in the form of a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:17.280
of radical followers he's amassed. They detonate&nbsp;
bombs he's planted on Gotham's reservoir walls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.000
causing a devastating amount of water to pour&nbsp;
into the city. When Batman finally comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:26.720
realize the Riddler's terrible plot, fiery&nbsp;
explosions can be seen erupting throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:31.600
the city in a scene reminiscent of Bane's&nbsp;
assault on Gotham in "The Dark Knight Rises."

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:36.480
The results are horrifying, as residents&nbsp;
of Gotham clamor to reach higher ground and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:40.560
water sweeps through the streets. The&nbsp;
moment adds some serious tension when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.760
audiences sit back and realize&nbsp;
that the Riddler may have won.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:48.240
It's not often that the bad guy walks away as&nbsp;
the victor, but this villain managed to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.680
widespread destruction to Gotham. The film's&nbsp;
resolution settles on Batman understanding that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:59.040
he has to do whatever he can to help Gotham's&nbsp;
innocents in the face of unrepentant evil.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:04.240
With the Riddler locked up tight at Arkham,&nbsp;
he can no longer do any harm directly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:08.160
But he's covered himself by rallying&nbsp;
extremists to his cause through his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:13.120
online presence. With Gotham City flooding&nbsp;
and descending into mass panic and chaos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:19.120
the group seeks to assassinate a mayoral candidate&nbsp;
and kill as many as they can at a political rally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:24.400
The group positions themselves high up on a rafter&nbsp;
where they rain down a hail of bullets upon the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:29.520
innocents below. Batman, once again, makes a&nbsp;
grand entrance by demolishing the top of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:35.360
building with explosives and dropping onto the&nbsp;
rafter before engaging the Riddler's disciples.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:40.960
The Dark Knight pummels and defeats bad guy&nbsp;
after bad guy in a thrilling combat sequence.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:45.360
Still early in his career, Batman isn't&nbsp;
quite the near-perfect crimefighter we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.160
know he'll one day become after accruing years&nbsp;
of experience on the job. He's attacked from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:54.320
a blind spot and nearly falls off the&nbsp;
rafters. Surveying his surroundings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:59.040
he employs the use of smoke to distract his&nbsp;
enemies while he takes each down one by one.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:03.440
"The Batman" establishes Batman&nbsp;
and Catwoman's relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:07.360
well. Both have aided each other&nbsp;
and even saved each other's lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:12.000
Their paths are interconnected, and Selina&nbsp;
senses that when she kisses the Dark Knight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.720
She pleads with him to skip town with her before&nbsp;
realizing he's too fixated on helping build a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:21.680
better Gotham City. While she doesn't believe&nbsp;
the city will ever change, Batman has hope.

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:26.640
As the two set off on their own paths, they&nbsp;
speed away on their respective motorcycles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:30.240
Despite this scene coming at the tail&nbsp;
end of the film, it's the first moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:35.600
viewers can admire the Batcycle in all of its&nbsp;
simplicity and beauty. While bleak themes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:41.040
corruption and darkness pervade "The Batman,"&nbsp;
seeing the Caped Crusader and Catwoman racing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:46.080
down the road together side by side offers a&nbsp;
glimmer of hope and optimism for the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:49.440
Even though they ultimately go their&nbsp;
separate ways, it's hard to believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:53.440
Selina Kyle is out of Batman's life for good&nbsp;
after all they've been through together.

